The bash tool now kills the command's whole process group when it times out or is cancelled, not just bash. Build, all tests, clippy with -D warnings and the fmt check pass, and it's committed as efaeb3b2.

The bug: StreamingBashTool killed only bash on timeout or cancel. Anything else the command started (the other stages of a pipe, && lists, background jobs) kept running with no parent.

Before and after: two new tests failed before the fix and pass after it:
• `test_streaming_bash_timeout_kills_pipeline_stage runs sleep 77.4101 | cat; echo done` with a 1s timeout.
• `test_streaming_bash_cancel_kills_background_job runs sleep 77.4102 & wait` and cancels it at 500ms.

Before the fix, pgrep -f still found the sleep alive in both. After it, nothing is left.

The fix copies yoagent 0.25.2's BashTool, which I read first:
• The command starts in its own process group (process_group(0)).
• A `GroupKill guard sends SIGKILL` to the whole group on timeout, on cancel, and if the tool is abandoned mid-run.
• The guard is switched off when the command finishes on its own.

One deliberate difference: on timeout and cancel, yoyo signals the group before waiting on bash to finish dying. At that point bash's process id can't have been reused by anything else; yoagent relies on cleanup order instead.

Checks on the fix:
• **Sabotage test:** I disabled the group kill, and exactly the two new tests failed by name. After restoring it everything was green, with no sabotage marker left in the code.
• **Commands that finish normally:** a job the command deliberately backgrounds (`cmd >log 2>&1 &`) keeps running, with the output checked byte-for-byte. Non-zero exit codes are still reported. Both are pinned by tests.
• **Ctrl+C:** because the command has its own group, it no longer gets the terminal's interrupt signal directly. I checked by reading the code that Ctrl+C in `prompt.rs` cancels the agent, which triggers the tool's cancel branch, which now kills the group. prompt.rs needed no change.

Files outside the planned three:
• The module-size test failed because src/tools.rs was too big. It was already over its recorded limit before this task: 5128 lines against 5053, inside the 100-line allowance. So I moved the new tests to a new file, src/tools_group_kill_tests.rs, registered in src/main.rs.
• I raised the recorded size for src/tools.rs to 5202 in tests/module_size.rs, with the reason written next to it.
• `ARCHITECTURE.md has a new dated entry for src/tools.rs`.

Still not fixed:
• Sub-agents use yoagent's own BashTool, which keeps the old behaviour (kill bash only) until yoyo upgrades to yoagent 0.25.2. Until then the main agent and its sub-agents differ on what a timeout kills.
• /run, `! and /bg` start their own processes and weren't changed.

One check I couldn't explain: right after the full test run, a pgrep for the test sleeps returned one process id. It had exited by the time I looked it up a moment later, so I couldn't tell which test it came from. Three more runs of the new tests each left no survivors.
